  .kpi-card .unit {{ font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--muted); }}

  section {{ padding: 40px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }}
  section:last-of-type {{ border-bottom: none; }}
  h2 {{ font-size: 1.6rem; color: #fff; margin-bottom: 20px; padding-bottom: 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent); display: inline-block; }}
  h3 {{ font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--accent2); margin: 24px 0 12px; }}
  p {{ color: var(--text); margin-bottom: 16px; }}
  .chart-box {{ background: var(--card); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px; margin: 20px 0; border: 1px solid var(--border); }}
  .chart-box canvas {{ width: 100%; max-height: 450px; }}

  .decade-grid {{
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(340px, 1fr));
    gap: 20px; margin: 20px 0;
  }}
  .decade-card {{
    background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 24px;
  }}
  .decade-card h3 {{ margin-top: 0; color: var(--accent); }}
  .decade-stats {{
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px; margin: 12px 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }}
  .decade-stats .stat-label {{ color: var(--muted); }}
  .decade-stats .stat-value {{ color: #fff; font-weight: 600; text-align: right; }}

  .shock-table {{ width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }}
  .shock-table th {{ background: var(--card); color: var(--muted); text-align: left; padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: 600; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }}
  .shock-table td {{ padding: 10px 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }}
  .shock-table .positive {{ color: var(--green); }}
  .shock-table .negative {{ color: var(--red); }}

  .stat-grid {{
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(160px, 1fr));
    gap: 12px; margin: 20px 0;
  }}
  .stat-item {{
    background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 16px; text-align: center;
  }}
  .stat-item .s-label {{ color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.75rem; text-transform: uppercase; }}
  .stat-item .s-value {{ color: #fff; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 4px; }}

  footer {{ background: var(--card); padding: 30px 0; text-align: center; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.8rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }}
  .highlight {{ color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }}

  @media (max-width: 768px) {{
    header h1 {{ font-size: 1.6rem; }}
    .kpi-grid {{ grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }}
    .decade-grid {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; }}
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Global Crude Oil Price Trends</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A Comprehensive Historical Analysis — January 1970 to March 2026</p>
    <p class="meta">Generated: {datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y')} &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data Source: Kaggle Global Fuel Price Trends &nbsp;|&nbsp; 675 Monthly Observations</p>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">

  <!-- Key KPIs -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Overall Price Range</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt(price_range)}</div>
      <div class="unit">USD/bbl (${fmt(price_min)}–${fmt(price_max)})</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">CAGR (Annualized)</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt(cagr, 1)}%</div>
      <div class="unit">Jan 1970 → Mar 2026 ({years:.1f} years)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Max MoM Change (Abs)</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt(max_mom_abs, 1)}</div>
      <div class="unit">USD/bbl — {max_mom_abs_date.strftime('%b %Y')}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Max MoM Change (%)</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt(max_mom_pct, 1)}%</div>
      <div class="unit">{max_mom_pct_date.strftime('%b %Y')}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Highest Avg Decade</div>
      <div class="value">{int(decade_with_highest_avg['Decade'])}s</div>
      <div class="unit">${fmt(decade_with_highest_avg['Avg_Price'])}/bbl average</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Price Std Deviation</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt(price_std)}</div>
      <div class="unit">USD/bbl — measure of dispersion</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: Executive Overview -->
  <section id="executive-overview">
    <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
    <p>
      This report analyzes <strong>675 monthly observations</strong> of global crude oil prices from January 1970 through
      March 2026 — a span of <strong>{years:.1f} years</strong>. The dataset captures one of the most eventful periods
      in commodity market history, encompassing multiple supply shocks, demand booms, financial crises, and a global pandemic.
    </p>
    <p>
      Over this period, crude oil prices have risen from <strong>${first_price:.2f}</strong>/bbl in January 1970 to
      <strong>${last_price:.2f}</strong>/bbl in March 2026, representing a compound annual growth rate of
      <strong>{cagr:.1f}%</strong>. However, this headline figure masks extraordinary volatility: the all-time
      nominal high of <strong>${price_max:.2f}</strong>/bbl was reached in {df.loc[df['Price'].idxmax(), 'Date'].strftime('%B %Y')},
      while the all-time low remained at <strong>${price_min:.2f}</strong>/bbl (the starting price in 1970).
    </p>
    <p>
      The key structural insight is that crude oil prices have transitioned through distinct regimes — from the stable
      sub-$3 era of the early 1970s, through the shock-prone 1970s–1980s, a period of relative calm in the 1990s,
      the super-cycle of the 2000s, the shale-driven volatility of the 2010s, and the pandemic/geopolitical turbulence
      of the 2020s. The standard deviation of <strong>${price_std:.2f}</strong>/bbl underscores the persistent
      volatility embedded in global energy markets.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Long-Term Price Trajectory -->
  <section id="long-term-trend">
    <h2>Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2>
    <p>
      The full time series reveals a structural break from the low-price stability of the early 1970s.
      After averaging below $3/bbl from 1970–1973, prices entered a permanently higher and more volatile regime.
      The chart below maps the complete journey — five decades of price discovery in global energy markets.
    </p>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="monthlyChart"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted); margin-top:8px;">
      * Dashed vertical lines mark major geopolitical and economic events. The shaded bands highlight decade boundaries.
    </p>
    <p>
      The upward-sloping regression trend (shown in amber) confirms the secular bull trend, but the path has been
      anything but linear. The <strong>2008 super-spike</strong> stands as the most dramatic peak, followed by the
      <strong>2020 COVID-19 collapse</strong> as the sharpest trough of the modern era. The post-2022 period suggests
      a new equilibrium forming in the $55–90 range, though volatility persists.
    </p>

    <h3>Annual Price Ranges</h3>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="yearlyChart"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p>
      The annual view highlights the widening price bands that accompanied the structural shift. From 1970–1973,
      the annual range was effectively zero; by the 2000s, intra-year swings of $50+ became common.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: Decade-by-Decade Breakdown -->
  <section id="decade-by-decade">
    <h2>Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
    <p>
      Grouping the data by decade reveals the evolution of pricing regimes. Each decade is characterized by
      distinct average price levels, volatility profiles, and event-driven dynamics.
    </p>

    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="decadeBarChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="decade-grid">
      {''.join(f'''
      <div class="decade-card">
        <h3>{nar['title']}</h3>
        <div class="decade-stats">
          <span class="stat-label">Average Price</span><span class="stat-value">${decades[decades["Decade"]==d]["Avg_Price"].values[0]:.2f}</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Median Price</span><span class="stat-value">${decades[decades["Decade"]==d]["Median_Price"].values[0]:.2f}</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Std Deviation</span><span class="stat-value">${decades[decades["Decade"]==d]["Std_Dev"].values[0]:.2f}</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Min / Max</span><span class="stat-value">${decades[decades["Decade"]==d]["Min_Price"].values[0]:.2f} / ${decades[decades["Decade"]==d]["Max_Price"].values[0]:.2f}</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Observations</span><span class="stat-value">{int(decades[decades["Decade"]==d]["Count"].values[0])}</span>
        </div>
        <p>{nar['text']}</p>
      </div>
      ''' for d, nar in sorted(decade_narratives.items()))}
    </div>

    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="decadeVolatilityChart"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);">
      Bar chart shows average price per decade; error bars represent one standard deviation.
      The 2000s combined the highest average with the highest volatility.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: Price Volatility & Major Shocks -->
  <section id="volatility-and-shocks">
    <h2>Price Volatility &amp; Major Shocks</h2>
    <p>
      Crude oil is among the most volatile major commodities. This section quantifies the
      largest price moves and ties them to specific historical events.
    </p>

    <h3>Month-over-Month Price Changes</h3>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="momChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Year-over-Year Price Changes</h3>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="yoyChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Top 10 Largest Monthly Moves</h3>
    <table class="shock-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Date</th><th>Price ($)</th><th>MoM Change ($)</th><th>MoM Change (%)</th><th>Context</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
"""

# Top 10 largest absolute MoM moves
top_mom = df.nlargest(10, 'MoM_Abs')[['Date', 'Price', 'MoM_Abs', 'MoM_Pct']].copy()
for _, row in top_mom.iterrows():
    d = row['Date'].strftime('%b %Y')
    p = row['Price']
    abs_chg = row['MoM_Abs']
    pct_chg = row['MoM_Pct']
    cls = 'positive' if abs_chg > 0 else 'negative'
    html += f"""        <tr><td>{d}</td><td>\${p:,.2f}</td><td class="{cls}">{abs_chg:+,.2f}</td><td class="{cls}">{pct_chg:+.1f}%</td><td>Major monthly swing</td></tr>\n"""

html += """      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Top 10 Largest Monthly Declines</h3>
    <table class="shock-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Date</th><th>Price ($)</th><th>MoM Change ($)</th><th>MoM Change (%)</th><th>Context</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
"""

top_declines = df.nsmallest(10, 'MoM_Abs')[['Date', 'Price', 'MoM_Abs', 'MoM_Pct']].copy()
for _, row in top_declines.iterrows():
    d = row['Date'].strftime('%b %Y')
    p = row['Price']
    abs_chg = row['MoM_Abs']
    pct_chg = row['MoM_Pct']
    cls = 'positive' if abs_chg > 0 else 'negative'
    # Try to assign context
    y = row['Date'].year
    m = row['Date'].month
    ctx = 'Major monthly decline'
    if y == 2020 and m in [3, 4]:
        ctx = 'COVID-19 demand shock'
    elif y == 2008 and m >= 9:
        ctx = 'Global financial crisis'
    elif y == 2014 and m >= 10:
        ctx = 'OPEC price war / shale glut'
    elif y == 1986:
        ctx = '1986 oil price collapse'
    html += f"""        <tr><td>{d}</td><td>\${p:,.2f}</td><td class="{cls}">{abs_chg:+,.2f}</td><td class="{cls}">{pct_chg:+.1f}%</td><td>{ctx}</td></tr>\n"""

html += """      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Statistical Summary & Distribution -->
  <section id="statistical-summary">
    <h2>Statistical Summary &amp; Distribution</h2>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">Mean</div><div class="s-value">$""" + fmt(price_mean) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">Median</div><div class="s-value">$""" + fmt(price_median) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">Std Deviation</div><div class="s-value">$""" + fmt(price_std) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">Minimum</div><div class="s-value">$""" + fmt(price_min) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">Maximum</div><div class="s-value">$""" + fmt(price_max) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">Range</div><div class="s-value">$""" + fmt(price_range) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">25th Percentile</div><div class="s-value">$""" + fmt(price_p25) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">75th Percentile</div><div class="s-value">$""" + fmt(price_p75) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">Skewness</div><div class="s-value">""" + fmt(df['Price'].skew(), 2) + """</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="s-label">Kurtosis</div><div class="s-value">""" + fmt(df['Price'].kurtosis(), 2) + """</div></div>
    </div>

    <p>
      The distribution of crude oil prices is <strong>positively skewed</strong> (skewness: """ + fmt(df['Price'].skew(), 2) + """),
      meaning the right tail is heavier — extreme high prices are more common than extreme low prices relative to the mean.
      The <strong>kurtosis</strong> of """ + fmt(df['Price'].kurtosis(), 2) + """ indicates a leptokurtic distribution: fatter
      tails than a normal distribution, confirming the prevalence of outlier price events. The median (""" + fmt(price_median) + """)
      lies well below the mean (""" + fmt(price_mean) + """), a consequence of the powerful price spikes that pull the average upward.
    </p>

    <h3>Price Distribution Histogram</h3>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="distChart"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);">
      The histogram groups prices into $5/bbl bins. The distribution's multi-modal nature reflects distinct price regimes across decades.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 6: Outlook & Conclusions -->
  <section id="outlook-and-conclusions">
    <h2>Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>

    <h3>Recent Price Trends (2023–2026)</h3>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="recentChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <p>
      The most recent data point in the dataset — <strong>${last_price:.2f}/bbl in March 2026</strong> —
      suggests a notable uptick from the preceding months, where prices had drifted below $60.
      This comes after the post-Ukraine-invasion normalization that saw crude retreat from the
      """ + fmt(df[(df['Year'] == 2022)]['Price'].max(), 2) + """/bbl highs of mid-2022.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Key conclusions from this analysis:</strong></p>
    <ul style="margin-left:20px; margin-bottom:16px;">
      <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
        <strong>Structural regime change is permanent.</strong> The pre-1973 era of stable prices below $3/bbl
        has never returned. Even the "low" prices of the 1990s ($15–20/bbl) were 5–10× higher than early-1970s levels.
      </li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
        <strong>Volatility is the defining feature.</strong> With a standard deviation of """ + fmt(price_std) + """/bbl
        and maximum monthly swings exceeding """ + fmt(max_mom_abs, 1) + """/bbl, crude oil is fundamentally
        a volatile asset. The largest moves cluster around geopolitical crises and demand shocks.
      </li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
        <strong>The """ + f"{int(decade_with_highest_avg['Decade'])}s" + """ hold the record for highest average prices</strong>
        at """ + fmt(decade_with_highest_avg['Avg_Price']) + """/bbl, driven by the China demand super-cycle
        and culminating in the 2008 all-time high.
      </li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
        <strong>The long-run CAGR of """ + fmt(cagr, 1) + """%</strong> from 1970 to 2026 includes both
        real demand growth and dollar inflation. Analysts should apply CPI adjustments for real-price comparisons.
      </li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">
        <strong>The 2020s are shaping up as a transition decade.</strong> The pandemic shock, war-driven spike,
        and subsequent moderation reflect the tension between traditional supply-demand fundamentals and
        the emerging energy transition narrative.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Data caveat:</strong> Observations beyond mid-2024 may include projected or forecast values.
      The March 2026 reading of """ + fmt(last_price) + """/bbl, in particular, should be interpreted with
      this in mind. All statistics are computed from nominal (not inflation-adjusted) US dollar prices.
    </p>
  </section>

</div>

<footer>
  <div class="container">
    <p>Report generated """ + datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y') + """ &nbsp;|&nbsp;
    Data: Kaggle Global Fuel Price Trends (minahilfatima12328) &nbsp;|&nbsp;
    Analysis: Python / pandas / Chart.js</p>
    <p style="margin-top:4px;">Prices are nominal USD/bbl. Not inflation-adjusted.</p>
